 ],
 "literal": "The red-legged female of the vankā bird that crossed, / because her mate fell as she attempted flight, not seeing her husband, / calls out repeatedly in a short, flute-like voice. / Without saying that the narrow paths full of hills are difficult, / leaving behind the beloved who is hard to forget, / saying 'I will depart' is indeed the end of youth here.",
 "translation": "The red-legged female of the vankā bird, whose mate fell and died as they took flight, searches in vain. Not finding her husband, she cries out repeatedly in a brief, flute-like voice. Without considering how difficult the narrow, hilly paths are, leaving behind my beloved who is so hard to forget, and saying 'I will depart'—surely, that would be the very end of youth.",
 "modern": "பறந்து சென்ற செங்கால்களைக் கொண்ட பெண் வங்காப் பறவை, தன் ஆண் பறவை பறக்க முயன்று கீழே விழுந்து இறந்ததால், தன் கணவனைக் காணாமல், குழலின் ஓசை போன்ற குரலில் மீண்டும் மீண்டும் அலறுகிறது. மலைகள் நிறைந்த குறுகிய வழிகள் கடப்பதற்கு அரியவை என்று எண்ணாமல், மறக்க முடியாத என் காதலியைத் தனியே தவிக்கவிட்டு, நான் பொருள் தேடப் பிரிந்து செல்வேன் என்று கூறுவது, இந்த இளமைப் பருவத்திற்குச் செய்யும் முடிவாகவே இருக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "The hero is speaking to himself when he contemplates leaving his beloved to earn wealth. He observes the grief of a female vankā bird whose mate has died, crying out in distress. This poignant scene reminds him of the pain of separation. He realizes that leaving his unforgettable beloved behind to traverse difficult mountain paths would destroy their youth and love. The image of the mourning bird mirrors the potential grief of the heroine, perfectly fitting the pālai landscape of separation.",
